From 403068e8d39ca8cd83d48b2529fa094b1d736909 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 00:26:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] ELFCodeLoader: emulate /proc/self/cmdline when the
 kernel can't be remapped

The loader points the kernel's arg_start/arg_end at the guest's argument
strings with prctl(PR_SET_MM_MAP), which needs CONFIG_CHECKPOINT_RESTORE.
Without it (the 64K POWER9 kernel) the prctl fails with EPERM in every
process, "Failed to remap /proc/pid/cmdline data" is logged at error
level, and a guest reading /proc/self/cmdline gets FEX's command line
instead of its own.

The loader now records the argument area and whether the remap worked,
and CodeLoader exposes it. When it didn't, EmulatedFDManager serves
/proc/self/cmdline and /proc/thread-self/cmdline from the guest's
strings, read at open time so a changed argv[0] shows. The failure is
logged at info level.

The table lookup also maps /proc/<own pid>/... to /proc/self/...: an
opened /proc/self file reads back under the numeric pid, and after a
fork that pid is no longer the one the table was keyed with at start-up.
This also covers maps, smaps and auxv in forked children.

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, a static x86-64 program that
prints /proc/self/cmdline, run as `FEX ./cmdline64 one "two words"`:
  before: fx86-before/FEX|./cmdline64|one|two words|
  after:  ./cmdline64|one|two words|
Linux prints the latter.

Still not covered: another process's /proc/<pid>/cmdline shows that
process's FEX command line.

Found in POWERarm.
